 First discard lower points and lower arcs when reconstructing the upper permutation. Its upper arch system is complete even if some lower arcs are open. The rightmost upper chain is a connected component. No upper arc can join two distinct intervening gaps without crossing a chain arc; an arc spanning the entire component would enclose its terminal point and end farther right, which is impossible. Nor can an additional arc enter a chain point, since each point has at most one incoming and one outgoing arc. The upper projection therefore has the stated initial and enclosed subobjects. The inverse in Proposition 3.2 reconstructs exactly its 213-avoiding upper permutation.
 
-Along the chain, successive values differ by one. In particular no other upper value lies numerically between any successive pair, and the chain values form one interval. For a point x inside the gap between ti and t(i+1), x is either below both endpoints or above both. The former possibility would make the position-ordered triple (ti,x,t(i+1)) an occurrence of 213. Hence x is above both, and consecutiveness of the whole chain interval puts x above the entire chain. For two nonempty enclosed subobjects Bi,Bj with 1<=i<j<m, choose arbitrary upper points x in Bi and y in Bj. The chain point t(i+1) lies between them in position and below both in value. If x<y, the triple (x,t(i+1),y) is 213. Thus x>y, proving the reverse value order for every pair of enclosed subobjects, not just their extrema. The initial upper subobject B0 is separate from the chain component. Concatenation becomes skew sum under Pi_213, so all its upper values exceed those of the entire terminal component, including the enclosed subobjects. Empty upper subobjects cause no exception to these comparisons.
